---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/home.css';
import CalculatorDirectory from '../components/CalculatorDirectory';
import CountrySection from '../components/CountrySection.astro';
---
<Base title="Money calculators that show their working | CalcsDesk" description="Explore limited US tax, UK salary, UAE gratuity and India regime betas with visible working and source references. Planned tools are clearly labelled.">
  <CalculatorDirectory client:load />
  <CountrySection />
  <section class="home-guide" id="choose-tool" aria-labelledby="choose-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">A useful starting point</p><h2 id="choose-title">Choose a calculation you can follow.</h2></div><span class="section-caption">Release status checked 2 November 2026</span></div>
    <p class="home-guide-intro">CalcsDesk is a growing collection of money calculators with visible working. The interactive collection has limited US federal income-tax, UK monthly-salary, UAE gratuity and India salary-regime betas. Use the matching tool to explore a supported case, read its assumptions and inspect the calculation. Other entries are plans, not finished calculators. Each beta still has review checks outstanding.</p>
    <div class="home-guide-grid">
      <article><span class="micro">01 / Start with the available tool</span><h3>Try the federal tax beta.</h3><p>The <a href="/us/tax/income-tax-calculator/">US federal calculator</a> accepts annual wages and a filing status. For a joint return, it provides a separate spouse-wage field. Its result separates regular federal income tax from employee Social Security and Medicare. The page explains the narrow wage-only case it supports and the adjustments it does not model. Read that scope before deciding whether its estimate is useful for your situation.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">02 / Understand the labels</span><h3>Beta and preparation mean different things.</h3><p>An interactive beta produces a result, but still has release checks outstanding. A tool in preparation shows its intended scope without calculating anything. The directory starts with working betas only. Turn on “Show tools in preparation” if you want to browse the build plan. Choosing a country without a beta will tell you that clearly instead of presenting a planned page as a working tool.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">03 / Inspect the working</span><h3>Read the breakdown beside the number.</h3><p>The federal beta shows the entered wage amount, deduction used and resulting taxable income. Its bracket table explains how the modeled regular income tax is assembled. Payroll-tax lines remain separate. You can compare the total with the individual pieces rather than relying on a single unexplained figure. For this federal tool, the annual view is the underlying estimate and its monthly view divides that annual result into an average. UK salary, UAE gratuity and India regime comparison use their own stated time bases; read those separately.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">04 / Match the question</span><h3>A tax estimate is a specific task.</h3><p>The federal tax beta is not the planned US paycheck calculator. It does not use W-4 settings, state or local taxes, benefit deductions or amounts already withheld. Its remainder is therefore not presented as take-home pay or a refund. If you need one of those answers, the tool’s limits explain why this first version cannot provide it. The planned paycheck entry remains labelled as preparation.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">05 / Check the reference</span><h3>Look for sources and review status.</h3><p>Each beta links to official publications behind its rules and records when the sources were checked. Source checking, automated testing and professional review are different steps. The pages distinguish completed component checks from pending human review and full release checks. Our <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> explains those gates, and the <a href="/team/">review-status page</a> describes the current position without implying that an expert panel has signed off.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">06 / Keep control of your figures</span><h3>Enter, inspect, then choose what to share.</h3><p>The beta calculators work in your browser without a financial-input submission endpoint or automatic wage storage. The federal beta’s requested share link includes the entered amounts and filing status in its URL fragment, so send it only to people you intend to see those details. Print is available on the federal and UK betas if you prefer a personal record. Neither action turns the estimate into a verified tax return.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">07 / Explore by place</span><h3>Use country pages as a clear map.</h3><p>The country cards lead to collections for the United States, United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, United Arab Emirates and India. They describe the intended tools and their current availability. The US, UK, UAE and India collections link to their limited betas; Canada and Australia currently show tools in preparation. They help you see the direction of the site without suggesting that every country already has an operational calculator.</p></article>
      <article><span class="micro">08 / Follow the development</span><h3>See what changed and what is still missing.</h3><p>The <a href="/updates/">public update log</a> records actual releases and meaningful changes. Our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> describes how sources, AI assistance and review claims are handled. If a result looks questionable, keep the assumptions and official reference in view. The <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a> explains the checking process and the current reporting-channel status. We do not present an inactive contact route as a working support service.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="method-strip" aria-labelledby="approach-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">The CalcsDesk approach</p><h2 id="approach-title">Know what goes into your number.</h2><p>A useful result should be easy to follow, question and check.</p><a class="text-link" href="/methodology/">Read the methodology →</a></div><ol><li><span>01 / Understand</span><h3>Start with your situation.</h3><p>Country, region and assumptions come first.</p></li><li><span>02 / Explore</span><h3>Follow the working.</h3><p>Each step belongs beside the final number.</p></li><li><span>03 / Check</span><h3>Look behind the result.</h3><p>Official sources and verified rates, before a tool goes live.</p></li></ol></section>
</Base>
